**Read:** [The fluid mechanics of aerosols — Goodarz Ahmadi, Clarkson](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0), the turbulent-flow and particle-measurement topics; revisit [Reynolds averaging, stress transport and the closure problem](../../../physics/fluid-dynamics/turbulence/Reynolds-averaging-stress-transport-and-the-closure-problem.lingua#r_2WR3KYF31R7RJSN20P6PKS0PM0) and its complete turbulence readings.

Averaging a transport equation introduces correlations between fluctuating velocity and concentration. Particle inertia can further separate particle statistics from those of the fluid. A mean streamline plot does not show every event that transports particles toward a wall or into a protected region.

An eddy diffusivity is a closure for selected averaged behavior, not the molecular Brownian diffusivity. Near-wall anisotropy, gradients in turbulence, particle response and geometry can make a constant scalar approximation inadequate. A detailed simulation still needs appropriate injection, boundary and sampling models.

**Work:** Derive the averaged concentration flux and identify the unclosed term. Compare a molecular-diffusion model, an eddy-diffusivity model and a resolved or stochastic particle model for one documented flow. Explain which observations would test the deposition prediction, not only the mean air speed.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
